        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Spacelab 2 (SL-2) was the third flight of Spacelab, the versatile space laboratory developed for NASA by the European Space Agency (ESA) which is designed to fit in the payload bay of the Space Shuttle.  The SL-2 crew consisted of Commander Gordon Fullerton, Pilot Roy D. Bridges, Mission Specialists F. Story Musgrave, Anthony W. England, and Karl G. Heinize, and Payload Specialists Loren W. Acton and John-David F. Bartoe. Each Spacelab mission has a unique design appropriate to the mission's goals.  A number of Spacelab configurations can be assembled from various standardized parts, such as habitable modules, an igloo and exposed platforms called pallets.  For the first time, the pallet only configuration was to be used in SL-2; three pallets, an Instrument Pointing System, and a special support structure were the main components.  The primary objectives of the SL-2 mission were to verify that the pallet-only configuration was satisfactory for observations and research.  <P>Spacelab 2 carried 13 investigations in 7 scientific disciplines:  solar physics, atmospheric physics, plasma physics, high-energy astrophysics, infrared astronomy, technology research and life sciences.  Some of these areas overlapped, and several instruments worked together to study the same phenomena with different techniques.  Although ESA provided engineering support because this mission was a test flight and part of the Spacelab development effort, the payload was not a joint ESA/NASA effort.<P>Two life sciences experiments were performed during SL-2 in the middeck of the Orbiter crew compartment, since the Spacelab was in the pallet-only mode.  The Vitamin D Metabolites and Bone Demineralization investigation measured the vitamin D metabolite levels of crewmembers to gain information on the causes of bone demineralization (the loss of bone density) and mineral imbalances that occur during prolonged space flight.  The biological investigation, Gravity-Influenced Lignification In Higher Plants, attempted to determine the effects of microgravity upon the production of lignin (a material that provides structural support) in higher plants.<P>A payload called the Plant Carry On Container (PCOC) was also flown on the STS-51F mission.  This payload was flown by students participating in the 1985 Space Life Sciences Training Program.  Several objectives were to be accomplished, including teaching the students how to use scientific method to answer scientific questions, teaching them what is involved in preparing an experiment to fly in the Space Shuttle middeck, and acquainting them with NASA Space Shuttle procedures.<P>Other life sciences experiments performed during the STS-51F mission were those classified as Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs).  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>Instruments on the three Spacelab pallets were also evaluated during this mission.  On the first pallet, three solar instruments and one atmospheric instrument were mounted on the Instrument Pointing System, an innovative pointing and navigating device developed by ESA which is capable of aiming the Shuttle instruments for maneuvering the Shuttle or conducting astronomy experiments more accurately than could be done before its development.  One plasma physics instrument, an electron beam generator, was also mounted on the first pallet.  The second Spacelab pallet held a large double x-ray  telescope and three plasma physics detectors.  The last pallet supported an infrared telescope, a superfluid helium technology experiment and a small plasma diagnostics satellite.  At the end of the pallet train, a cosmic ray detector was mounted on a specially designed support structure that was tilted to increase detector exposure to space.  The crewmembers controlled the pallet instruments via computer keyboards located in the flight deck.<P>The Spacelab 2 mission successfully demonstrated the ability of the pallet-only mode to provide a unique capability for Spacelab?s scientific users.  Spacelab 2 was the final mission of the original trio of Spacelab missions, proving the usefulness and flexibility of the orbiting laboratory and providing scientists from all over the world with a new and intriguing environment in which to conduct research.<END>
